 required to  produce that compose that then obviously  I have to be there in pre-production and  then as the process of filmmaking  continues you know you can be involved  in you can go down to the set you can  see how the directors working with  actors you can go into the cutting room  you can see dailies and then you start  looking at assemblies and the ideas that  you've had and the things that you've  been working on you can apply them and  you can rework them and you can refine  them it's a constant ongoing thing  that's right deal with situation but  what equally can happen is that you get  a call saying we need this delivered in  two weeks time can you do it I tend to  work with the same people over and over  and over and I have a group of four or  five directors that I really love and  consider great friends and so we'll be  talking about these things a year or two  before anything happens the amount of  work that you have and the amount of  time that you have will match ultimately  you know I mean I've done films where  I've been given two or three weeks to do  the entire thing from start to finish  and I've managed to do it and it's taken  me two or three weeks I've done films  where I've been told I've got five  months to do it and it's taken me five  months to do it so I suppose the only  difference is in that five month period  there's probably going to be a bit of  procrastination at the front end and a  little bit of daytime telly watching and  Doritos eating I'm a very organised  composer I come in at the same time  every single day I come in between the  eight o clock and half past eight I  usually have a half an hour break and I  walk to anything from five to seven  depending on the schedule and I never  work Saturdays Sundays if I can help it  so I'm very very disciplined I like to  treat it sort of like it's my work and I  get up in the morning I get the kids off  to school and then I go upstairs to my  writing room and that's what I do and  I'm there till 5:30 and then I'm done  that's a time where I can kind of just  think about what I was working on or or  experience other things and maybe get  new inspirations or new new ideas I  think part of the thing about writing a  film which is got a lot of me  you know and it's not unusual now for  sort of big action films they have like  90 to 100 minutes of music yet  you know it's a lot of dots your first  couple of weeks when you're writing that  you kind of set the template for for  what it is that you're making and you  try and do something which feels a  little bit different each time but but  as you go further and further in you  realize that that's the language of the  film and to a certain extent it becomes  a little bit simpler the the later you  get into the process because you're not  going to try and do something completely  different a minute 85 then you've been  doing for the first eighty four minutes  but the big pressure when you're doing  those kinds of films with that amount of  music is just the amount you have to  churn out in a day you know anything  from sort of two to five minutes of  music sometimes you know and that's not  always the best way to be doing it it's  a little bit of a scramble but then when  you get to the theater and you watch the  thing and you realize that oh there are  loads of explosions or helicopters and  can't hear it anyway you know so that's  that's the other thing you have to think  about knowing when not to write you know  what has to adapt or die and see this -  - up-and-coming composers that you have  to be aware of what's out there you have  to ask and I work with younger people  who will challenge me and introduce with  new sounds and you can buy signs you can  you buy gear and and I have programmers  he'll twist sounds around and Jack Ryan  in particular and I spent four weeks  with a programmer creating new signs  before I even began you know I haven't  written out music since the last time I  that was Mission Possible 3 actually was  just at the last minute I decided I did  not like the love theme that I wrote and  I we were on the stage at Warner  Brothers and I just grabbed the piano  and wrote a new one there and I hand  wrote but that was kind of the last time  I think because the computer is now are  so fast and so convenient and you can  edit and change things it's so much  easier and it sort of eliminates a step  of communication between me and my team  you know I can then hand off a sketch  for the orchestra that is you know 75 80  % done  pretty much used computers from day one  the film now comes in digitally I sketch  a lot of things in in logic just on  Apple Macs but but then we move parts  into Sibelius and serve aliases the  prime sort of music part generating tool  when I did the Olympics we did quite a  lot of composition in Sibelius and you  can hear what you're doing and you can  see what you're doing and at that point  once we're happy with the music as it  stands it's always always now recall it  in Pro Tools you know the days of  getting a tape are long gone as I see to  two youngsters you can have all this  electronic knowledge but there will be a  limit to employment capabilities you  meet yourself more employable if you  have a thorough training in music if  that's possible and if you want to be  still working at 61 which I am so far  then you're pulling on all this  experience continually and certain  schools demand that kind of knowledge  but my job is to be sure I can do other  genres and I've been very fortunate I've  managed to write for every single genre  and if you stop tomorrow I would agree  have a good time  you

